# Lambda Service Development Rules

## Core Principles

### Data Access Strategy
- **Parse Server is the ONLY data access layer** - No direct MongoDB connections
- **Use Parse Client** - All database operations through Parse Server SDK
- **Master Key Operations** - Use master key for server-side operations
- **No Direct Database Access** - All data goes through Parse Server

### SQS Message Processing
- **Batch Processing** - Handle multiple messages efficiently
- **Error Handling** - Implement proper retry logic and dead letter queues
- **Idempotency** - Ensure operations can be safely retried
- **Logging** - Comprehensive logging for debugging and monitoring

### Event-Driven Architecture
- **Event Types** - Handle different event types (USER_SIGNUP, USER_LOGIN, etc.)
- **Workflow Creation** - Create appropriate workflow records for each event
- **Status Updates** - Update event status from "work" to "done"
- **Custom Records** - Create event-specific collections as needed

## Required Code Structure

### Lambda Handler Pattern
```javascript
exports.handler = async (event, context) => {
  console.log('🚀 Lambda function triggered');
  console.log('📥 Event:', JSON.stringify(event, null, 2));
  
  const results = [];
  const batchItemFailures = [];
  
  try {
    // Process each message in the batch
    for (const record of event.Records) {
      // Process individual message
    }
    
    // Return batch item failures if any messages failed
    if (batchItemFailures.length > 0) {
      return { batchItemFailures, results };
    }
    
    return { statusCode: 200, body: { results } };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('💥 Lambda function error:', error);
    throw error;
  }
};
```

### Parse Server Integration
```javascript
// Always use Parse Client, never direct database access
const parseClient = new ParseClient();

// Use master key for server-side operations
await parseClient.updateAuthEventStatus(eventId, 'done', requestId);

// Create custom collections as needed
await parseClient.createCustomRecord('UserOnboarding', {
  userId: message.userId,
  status: 'initiated',
  // ... other fields
});
```

### Error Handling Pattern
```javascript
try {
  // Operation
} catch (error) {
  console.error('❌ Error description:', error);
  
  // Don't fail entire batch for non-critical errors
  // Log and continue, or add to batch failures if critical
  if (isCriticalError(error)) {
    batchItemFailures.push({ itemIdentifier: record.messageId });
  }
}
```

## Testing Requirements

- **Minimum 80% coverage** for all business logic
- **Mock external dependencies** (Parse Server, SQS)
- **Test error scenarios** and edge cases
- **Test different event types** and message formats

## Performance Requirements

- **Target**: Process messages within 5 seconds
- **Timeout**: Lambda timeout set to 30 seconds
- **Batch Size**: Handle up to 10 messages per batch
- **Memory Usage**: Efficient parsing and minimal object creation

## Security Requirements

- **Parse Server master key** for all operations
- **No hardcoded credentials** in code
- **Input sanitization** for all message data
- **No sensitive data** in error logs

## Rule Enforcement Checklist

**BEFORE making any Lambda changes, verify:**

- [ ] **Parse Server is the ONLY data access layer** - No direct MongoDB connections
- [ ] **Parse Client is used** - All database operations through Parse SDK
- [ ] **Master key operations** - Server-side operations use master key
- [ ] **Proper error handling** - Errors are logged and handled gracefully
- [ ] **Batch processing** - Multiple messages are processed efficiently
- [ ] **Test coverage** - New functionality has adequate tests
- [ ] **Performance requirements** - Execution time and memory usage are optimized

**If any rule is violated, STOP and fix it before continuing.**
